 dict(pmid='42914907', doi='10.1016/j.jhazmat.2026.143869', journal='J Hazard Mater', short='Perez-Vizcaino PM10 sources Riotinto mining multi-time-resolution PMF 2026', title='Characterization of PM(10) sources in the Riotinto copper mining district (SW Spain) using multi-time-resolution receptor modelling.', sub=corpus.EXPO, design='Intensive field campaign, PMF source apportionment', pm='Hourly XRF elements and Aethalometer BC + daily filter PM10 chemistry', geo='Spain (Riotinto mining district)', endpoint='PM10 source contributions', tier='B', n='Year-long (July 2024-July 2025) campaign combining an hourly XRF metals monitor and an Aethalometer with daily high-volume quartz-filter chemistry, merged in multi-time-resolution PMF. Seven sources: waste rock 4.9, regional + traffic 3.6, open pit 1.6, combustion 1.2, aged marine 1.2, dust 1.1 and copper concentrate 0.7 ug/m3. Daily species identified traffic tracers and SOA that the hourly subset alone missed. Arsenic was the main geochemical anomaly. Limitation: one receptor site; multi-resolution PMF weights the daily data heavily and factor uncertainty (bootstrap/DISP) is not reported in the abstract. Relevance: medium - hourly XRF + BC is the reference tier that low-cost networks around mines would need to be anchored to for metal-rich dust.'),
 dict(pmid='', doi='10.5194/acp-26-14867-2026', journal='Atmos Chem Phys', short='Chen primary brown carbon China 2005-2020 trends radiative effect 2026', title='Long-term evolution and effects of primary brown carbon aerosol in China', sub=corpus.EXPO, design='Chemical transport modelling', pm='Modelled primary brown carbon (internal-mixing optical model), PM2.5', geo='China', endpoint='Surface and column PBrC, sector shares, direct radiative effect', tier='B', n='CTM simulation of primary BrC across China, 2005-2020, with an internal-mixing optical model. Mean surface PBrC 0.81 ugC/m3; residential, industrial and agricultural combustion contributed 57%, 22% and 18%, together 91% of column PBrC in 2010. PBrC fell 20.8% over the period vs 8.1% for PM2.5; direct radiative effect fell from +0.032 to +0.023 W/m2 (-25.7%), 84.2% anthropogenic. Limitation: BrC emission factors and absorption Angstrom exponents are poorly constrained, and evaluation against measured absorption is not described in the abstract. Relevance: medium - multi-wavelength absorption (BrC) channels on sensor nodes could test the residential-combustion dominance.'),
 dict(pmid='', doi='10.5194/acp-26-14917-2026', journal='Atmos Chem Phys', short='Srivastav ion particle size distribution NPF Indo-Gangetic Plain 2026', title='Ion and particle size distribution measurements in the Indo-Gangetic Plain: seasonal insights and challenges in new particle formation event characterization', sub=corpus.SENS, design='Long-term field measurement campaign', pm='Air-ion (0.8-42 nm) and particle (2.5-42 nm) size distributions', geo='India (Sonipat, Haryana)', endpoint='NPF event frequency, condensation sink, growth rates', tier='B', n='Ion and particle spectrometer measurements at a suburban IGP site, May-December 2023. NPF occurred on 30/35 valid days (86%) in May-June but 20/73 (28%) in winter/post-monsoon. Morning median condensation sink was lower on event days (0.0095 vs 0.0292 s-1). Growth rates rose with size, 14.7 nm/h (3-7 nm) to 19.0 nm/h (7-20 nm). Pollution plumes intermittently masked or distorted events, so both visual and automated classification and growth-rate fitting were unreliable. Limitation: no precursor gas (H2SO4, amines, HOMs) measurements, so mechanism is unconstrained; eight months at one site. Relevance: medium - in high-CS settings the UFP number seen by condensation or diffusion-charger sensors is plume- not nucleation-driven.'),
 dict(pmid='', doi='10.3390/atmos17111039', journal='Atmosphere', short='Kuye ultrafine particle respiratory deposition dose wood stoves UK homes 2026', title='Respiratory Deposition Dose of Ultrafine Particles from Improved Wood Stoves and Open Fireplaces', sub=corpus.OCC, design='Field measurement', pm='Indoor particle number concentration (1-min) with ICRP deposition model', geo='United Kingdom (four homes)', endpoint='Inhaled and respiratory-deposited UFP dose', tier='B', n='Exploratory measurements in four UK homes (Ecodesign stove, multifuel Ecodesign stove x2, open fireplace), 11-12 winter days each, 1-min PNC combined with age/sex/activity ventilation rates and the ICRP model. Daily inhaled dose 0.45-16.0 x10^9 particles/kg/day sitting and 2.37-73.2 x10^9 at moderate activity (4.5-5.3-fold higher). Child scenarios had 2.7-3.8x the adult body-mass-normalised dose; household mean deposition rate 1.50-5.99 x10^8 particles/min. Limitation: n=4 homes that differ simultaneously in appliance, fuel, room, ventilation and behaviour, so stove-type effects cannot be identified; doses are modelled from concentration, not measured. Relevance: medium - residential UFP is invisible to mass sensors; indoor PNC nodes are the relevant instrument.'),
